1:# uttt-zero — project plan
2:
3:Goal: train an AlphaZero-style agent for Ultimate Tic-Tac-Toe under the
4:closed-board / most-boards rules (= CodinGame rules), play it, then use it to
5:analyse the game. Research basis: `knowledge/01`–`06`.
6:
7:## 0. Findings that shape the plan (from the knowledge base)
8:
9:- **The variant is exactly CodinGame's** (`knowledge/01`): won or full boards
10:  are closed, a closed target gives a free move, 3 boards in a row wins,
11:  otherwise more won boards wins, equal counts = draw.
12:- **Unsolved and not solvable here** (`knowledge/06`): the 2020 "at most 43
13:  moves" paper solves Orlin's *original* rules (you must keep playing inside a
14:  won board), by hand, with no computer. The closed-board game's state space is
15:  ~10^33–10^38; the best solver (Elhage's DFPN) extrapolates to hundreds of
16:  millions of CPU-hours. A K=1-open-board endgame tablebase (~10^10 positions)
17:  *is* feasible and is the one exact component worth building.
18:- **Not moot** (`knowledge/03`): no public AlphaZero-style agent exists for the
19:  most-boards variant (the two that exist for CodinGame rules are private
20:  bots); nothing resembling a strategy analysis has been published for any
21:  variant. Best public baselines: uttt.ai (draw variant, 5M params) and
22:  SaltZero (draw variant, beat the #2 CodinGame bot).
23:- **Algorithm** (`knowledge/02`): Gumbel AlphaZero at ~32 sims/move, 6×64
24:  ResNet on 9×9 planes, WDL value head + local-board ownership auxiliary,
25:  D4 symmetry augmentation, no gating, lock-step vectorised search on the GPU.
26:- **Compute** (`knowledge/04`): comparable games (Hex, Othello, Go 9×9) reach
27:  strong play in 3–18 GPU-hours with pgx-style vectorised self-play; expect
28:  0.3–2 M games = hours to ~2 days on the 3090.
29:
30:## 1. Measured on this machine (2026-08-29)
31:
32:| Component | Result |
33:|---|---|
34:| Batched engine, 32k games, 3090 | 6.6–6.9 M game-steps/s incl. encoding |
35:| Net 6×64 fp16, batch 4096, 3090 | 17 ms/batch = 0.24 M positions/s |
36:| Net 4×64 fp16, batch 4096, 3090 | 10 ms/batch = 0.39 M positions/s |
37:| Net 6×64 fp16, batch 4096, 3060 | 33 ms/batch = 0.12 M positions/s |
38:| Tree ops per simulation, 4096 trees | 16–18 ms (uniform evaluator, no net) |
39:| Self-play, dev1 (v1 lock-step, eager) | 25–33 games/s ≈ 100 k games/h, GPU 20–35 % busy |
40:| Self-play, v2 eager (edge stats, 2 syncs/sim) | 42–60 games/s |
41:| **Self-play, v2 CUDA-graph mode** (`runs/v2a`) | **155–170 games/s ≈ 600 k games/h**, ~10 k positions/s, GPU 84 % busy |
42:
43:So 1 M self-play games ≈ 1.7 h in graph mode (was ~10 h in dev1).
44:
45:## 2. Phases
46:
47:### Phase A — pipeline (done)
48:- [x] Reference engine + tests (`uttt/game.py`, `tests/test_game.py`)
49:- [x] Batched tensor engine, encoding, D4 symmetries, cross-checked (`uttt/batch.py`)
50:- [x] ResNet with policy / WDL / ownership heads (`uttt/model.py`)
51:- [x] Batched MCTS: PUCT and Gumbel (`uttt/mcts.py`), tactical tests
52:- [x] Self-play, replay buffer, trainer, arena (`uttt/selfplay.py`, `train.py`, `arena.py`)
53:- [x] Smoke run; first real run `runs/dev1` (4096 games × 32 sims, 6×64, 200 iters)
54:
55:### Phase B — make it learn well (in progress)
56:- [x] dev1 finished (200 iterations, 820 k games, ~12 h): see `RESULTS-dev1.md`.
57:      Plateau from iteration ~90; root policy collapse; heavy opening duplication;
58:      evaluation cost a third of the wall-clock.
59:- [x] v2 pipeline (`NOTES-v2.md` §8): sync-free edge-statistics search, fused
60:      inference, continuous self-play, GPU buffer with duplicate down-weighting,
61:      sampled opening moves + root prior floor, margin head, LR schedule, game
62:      persistence, cheap fixed-anchor evaluation.
63:- [x] v2a run (`runs/v2a`, `RESULTS-v2a.md`): 789 k games in 2.75 h; +70 Elo over dev1's final net;
64:      corpus statistics (tiebreak share, decision phase, free-move tempo); opening table.
65:- [x] CUDA-graph search (155–220 games/s), symmetry-averaged evaluator, per-orbit deep opening search.
66:- [ ] v2b run (`runs/v2b`): exploration floor, progressive sims 32 → 48 → 64, anchors dev1 + v2a.
67:- [ ] Search-value mixing, bigger batches, wider net (6×96 / 8×128), ownership/margin ablation.
68:- [ ] Scale the net (6×96, 8×128) once 6×64 plateaus under v2.
69:
70:### Phase C — evaluation anchors
71:- Checkpoint Elo ladder (side-swapped matches, Bayesian Elo).
72:- Plain UCT with random rollouts at large budgets as a fixed classical anchor.
73:- Rebuild a CodinGame-Legend-grade bitboard MCTS (Numba) as an external anchor;
74:  optionally SaltZero / uttt.ai under *their* draw rules for calibration.
75:- Endgame tablebase (K = 1 open board) → exact labels for the value head.
76:
77:### Phase D — play against it
78:- Text / web UI (`play.py` first; a browser page later) with search budget,
79:  hints, and the agent's evaluation shown per move.
80:
81:### Phase E — analysis of the game (the real goal; `knowledge/05`)
82:1. Self-play corpus statistics: win rate by first move (15 symmetry orbits),
83:   share of games ending by line vs. count, closed-board counts by ply, free-move
84:   frequency and its value, which local boards are won first, centre control.
85:2. Opening value heatmaps and principal-variation trees from deep search
86:   (thousands of sims) at every first move / every 2-ply opening.
87:3. Counterfactual value probes: flip ownership of a local board, remove a
88:   free move, etc., and read the value-head delta — quantifies "the centre
89:   board is worth X", "a free move is worth Y".
90:4. Surprise mining: positions where the net's prior and the search disagree
91:   most — the tactical motifs the net has not internalised.
92:5. Ambitious: linear concept probes on the residual stream (McGrath et al.),
93:   with ablation checks, and distillation into a small rule set to test how
94:   much strength survives.
95:
96:## 3. Open questions for John
97:- Rule check: tie on board count = draw (as on CodinGame) — confirmed as the
98:  implemented rule; say if you play it differently.
99:- Are you happy for the 3090 to be busy for hours at a time? Runs resume from
100:  `latest.pt`, so they can be stopped and restarted freely.

