1:# v2 notes — review of the v1 pipeline and candidate optimisations
2:
3:Written 2026-08-29 while `runs/dev1` was training. Nothing here is implemented
4:yet. Items are ordered by expected payoff per hour of work. Numbers marked
5:*measured* come from `tests/diag_search.py` (run on the idle 3060, n=1024,
6:8 sims) and `tests/bench_net.py`.
7:
8:## 0. Correctness review (no changes needed for the running job)
9:
10:Re-read `batch.py`, `mcts.py`, `selfplay.py`, `train.py`, `arena.py`, `model.py`.
11:
12:- Gumbel search matches mctx step by step: Sequential-Halving table, root score
13:  `g + logits + σ(q̂)` restricted to `N == considered_visit`, interior rule
14:  `π' − N/(1+ΣN)`, completed-Q with the mixed value, final action = best among
15:  the most-visited, policy target `softmax(logits + σ(completed q̂))`. One
16:  deliberate deviation: min/max rescaling of q̂ uses legal actions only (mctx
17:  uses all actions) — harmless, arguably better.
18:- Symmetry pipeline is consistent end to end: `apply_symmetry` permutes cells
19:  with `SYM_CELL_INV[s]`, macro/ownership with `SYM_BOARD_INV[s]`, policy with
20:  `SYM_CELL_INV[s]`; verified by replaying transformed move sequences.
21:- Policy index conversion (grid ↔ engine order) is applied identically in
22:  `Evaluator` and `train_steps` (`logits[:, INV_PERM]`).
23:- Value/ownership class mapping `(1 − target).clamp(0,2)` → win 0 / draw 1 / loss 2.
24:- Terminal roots (finished games inside a lock-step batch) are handled: the
25:  selection loop stops immediately, no node is expanded, nothing is recorded.
26:- Things that are *not bugs but worth knowing*:
27:  - `c_scale = 0.1` follows mctx's `value_scale` default with q̂ rescaled to
28:    [0,1]; the paper's text quotes `c_scale = 1.0`. This is a 10× difference in
29:    how sharply the improved policy follows Q. A/B it (item 3.6).
30:  - PUCT mode's Dirichlet α = 1.0 is right for ~9 legal moves but too large for
31:    free-move positions (up to ~50 legal moves). Not used by dev1 (Gumbel).
32:  - One symmetry is drawn *per training batch*, not per sample (item 4.4).
33:  - `latest.pt` re-saves the whole replay buffer (~0.5 GB) every iteration.
34:
35:## 1. Search throughput — the dominant cost (expected 3–6× overall)
36:
37:*Measured*: per simulation, uniform evaluator, n=1024: **548 CUDA kernels,
38:45 host↔device synchronisations, GPU busy 1.2 ms, CPU 24.6 ms.** With the
39:6×64 net: 720 kernels, GPU busy 10.5 ms. At n=4096 the wall-clock is ~17 ms of
40:tree work + ~17 ms of network per simulation, and `nvidia-smi` shows ~33 %
41:utilisation during training. The tree work is almost entirely launch overhead
42:and sync stalls (Windows/WDDM launch latency is ~2–4× Linux's).
43:
44:1.1 **Remove every per-simulation sync** (largest single win, ~2× on tree ops).
45:    Sources, all in `mcts.py`:
46:    - `bool(active.any())` per depth level → bound the loop by a tracked
47:      `max_depth_seen + 1` (a tree's depth grows ≤ 1 per simulation) or by a
48:      fixed small D and treat the unfinished descent as the leaf.
49:    - `int(depth.max().item())` + `int(valid.sum())` per backup level → one
50:      vectorised backup: nodes `path[:, :D+1]`, `valid = j <= depth[:,None]`,
51:      `sign = (−1)^(depth−j)`, then a single `index_add_` on the flattened
52:      `(game*M + node)` index for `visit` and for `value_sum`.
53:    - `_put` boolean-mask assignments (7 fields × `nonzero`) → `torch.where`
54:      full-row writes: `s_cells[:, slot] = where(mask[:,None], cells, s_cells[:, slot])`.
55:    - `self.children[idx[need_expand], parent[need_expand], action[need_expand]] = i`
56:      → write for all rows with a dummy slot for non-expanding games, or
57:      `index_put_` on precomputed flat indices with a masked value.
58:    - `selfplay.py`: `res.policy[active]` etc. per ply → record everything and
59:      filter once at the end; `float(...)` of surprise per ply → accumulate on GPU.
60:1.2 **Edge-statistics layout.** Store `N[n,M,81]` and `W[n,M,81]` on the parent
61:    (from the parent's perspective) instead of gathering child rows through the
62:    `children` table. Selection becomes two gathers instead of five ops plus
63:    `where`; backup needs the action taken at each path step (`path_actions`).
64:    Cuts ~⅓ of the launches and removes the −W/N sign juggling.
65:1.3 **CUDA-graph the simulation.** Once 1.1 makes the simulation shape-static
66:    and sync-free (fixed depth loop D, e.g. 12, with truncation), capture
67:    selection + expansion + network forward + backup as one `torch.cuda.CUDAGraph`
68:    and replay it `n_sims` times per move; the Gumbel schedule column and the
69:    simulation index become static input tensors updated by `copy_`. Expected:
70:    tree work → ~1–2 ms/sim (its measured GPU time), leaving the net.
71:1.4 **Bigger batches once the tree work is cheap**: 8192–16384 parallel games
72:    make the run network-bound; positions/iteration scale accordingly, so
73:    also scale training steps (item 4).
74:1.5 **Continuous self-play instead of lock-step-to-the-last-game.** The batch
75:    currently runs until its *longest* game ends (up to ~75–81 plies) while the
76:    mean is 43–55; tail steps cost nearly as much as full steps because the
77:    cost is per simulation, not per live game. Reset finished games in place
78:    and run a fixed number of plies per iteration; assign outcomes per game id
79:    when each game ends (pgx/turbozero pattern). Expected ~1.4×.
80:1.6 **`torch.compile` via `triton-windows`** for the selection step (fuses the
81:    ~30 elementwise/gather ops per level into a handful). Optional if 1.3 works;
82:    cheaper to try first (pip install, `@torch.compile` on a function).
83:1.7 Windows settings to check before optimising: Hardware-accelerated GPU
84:    scheduling (Settings → Display → Graphics → Default graphics settings),
85:    and that the desktop is driven by the 3060, not the 3090.
86:
87:## 2. Network inference (expected ~1.5×)
88:
89:*Measured* on the 6×64 net, batch 1024 fp16 (per 8 sims): conv 27 ms,
90:**BatchNorm inference 15 ms (135 kernels)**, **NCHW↔NHWC conversions 15 ms
91:(324 kernels)**, elementwise 13 ms.
92:
93:2.1 **Fold BatchNorm into the conv weights** for the inference copy
94:    (`torch.nn.utils.fusion.fuse_conv_bn_eval`) — removes ~18 % of GPU time and
95:    ~17 kernels/sim.
96:2.2 **`channels_last` + a true `.half()` inference copy** (no autocast casts):
97:    kills the layout-conversion kernels (~18 %) and per-op cast launches.
98:    Refresh the inference copy from the training weights once per iteration.
99:2.3 The conv itself: cudnn's implicit-GEMM at 9×9 spatial is ~50 % of the 3090's
100:    fp16 peak — acceptable. A 4×64 net is ~1.7× faster (0.39 vs 0.24 M pos/s)
101:    if strength permits; decide after dev1 plateaus. TensorRT is the only path
102:    to substantially better small-conv kernels; not worth it yet.
103:2.4 `encode()` recomputes the legal mask that the evaluator also computes;
104:    pass it in. Build planes directly in fp16.
105:2.5 Overlap tree work and network on two CUDA streams (ping-pong halves of the
106:    batch) — only relevant if 1.3 leaves the CPU as the bottleneck.
107:
108:## 3. Learning efficiency (same games → stronger net)
109:
110:3.1 **Persist the games.** Save each iteration's move sequences (uint8, ≤81
111:    per game; 4096 games ≈ 330 KB) plus root values. This is the corpus for
112:    Phase E statistics *and* lets us replay training history. Cheap; do first.
113:3.2 **Score-margin auxiliary head** (won-board difference at the end, 17 bins
114:    from −8..+8, or a regression) — the tiebreak is decided by this quantity;
115:    KataGo's score head was its largest single data-efficiency gain.
116:3.3 **Value target = mix of game result z and search root value** (e.g. 0.5/0.5,
117:    or z for the last k plies only): lower-variance targets; standard in
118:    reproductions that report faster early learning. Root values are already
119:    computed; store them in the buffer.
120:3.4 **Replay window schedule**: start ~4 iterations, grow to ~20 as the net
121:    stabilises (Oracle, KataGo). Currently fixed at 2 M positions ≈ 10 iterations.
122:3.5 **Learning-rate schedule**: warm-up 200 steps, then drop ×0.1 twice when
123:    `eval_vs_prev` stops improving; or switch to AdamW 1e-3 for the small net.
124:3.6 **A/B `c_scale` 0.1 vs 1.0** and `m_considered` 16 vs 9 at 32 sims; then
125:    **progressive sims** 32 → 64 → 128 after the plateau.
126:3.7 **Opening diversity**: all 4096 games start from the empty board and only
127:    Gumbel noise separates them; log the first-move histogram and the fraction
128:    of duplicated positions in the buffer per ply. Options: start a fraction of
129:    games from buffer-sampled positions, or weight duplicated positions down.
130:3.8 Extra input planes (cheap): side-to-move is X (first-player parity),
131:    "free move" flag, won-board count difference broadcast. The count plane
132:    gives the tiebreak logic directly instead of having to be computed by convs.
133:3.9 Per-sample symmetry during training (currently one symmetry per batch).
134:3.10 Consider playout-cap randomisation only if we return to PUCT; with Gumbel
135:    at 32 sims the paper's argument for it mostly disappears.
136:
137:## 4. Training loop hygiene (small but free)
138:
139:4.1 GPU-resident replay buffer (2 M positions ≈ 0.55 GB) → no CPU gather + H2D
140:    per step; training is 54 ms/step for a 0.5 M-param net, which is mostly
141:    host overhead.
142:4.2 Hoist `INV_PERM.to(device)` / `SYM_BOARD_INV.to(device)` out of the step
143:    loop; accumulate loss metrics on the GPU; one `.item()` per iteration.
144:4.3 Save the replay buffer every N iterations (or keep only positions, no
145:    optimiser state) — `latest.pt` is ~0.5 GB every 2.5 min.
146:4.4 Per-sample symmetry (see 3.9).
147:4.5 Log: first-move histogram (15 orbits), policy entropy by ply, buffer
148:    duplicate rate, end-by-count share under *evaluation* play, time split
149:    (self-play / train / eval / save).
150:
151:## 5. Evaluation (needed before any strength claim)
152:
153:5.1 **Anchor pool + Bayesian Elo**: random, uniform-UCT at 64/256/1024 sims,
154:    every 10th checkpoint; run matches on the 3060 in a separate process so
155:    the 3090 never idles (evaluation is ~15 % of dev1's wall-clock).
156:5.2 **Paired openings**: the two side-swapped halves of a match should reuse
157:    the same random opening moves (seeded), halving variance.
158:5.3 **Exact labels**: a Numba alpha-beta for positions with ≤ 2 open boards
159:    (fast — few plies remain) → a fixed test set of ~10 k solved positions;
160:    report value-head accuracy and search accuracy on it every evaluation.
161:    Later the K = 1-open-board tablebase (knowledge/06).
162:5.4 **External anchor**: Numba bitboard MCTS with random rollouts, ~100 k
163:    rollouts/turn — the CodinGame-Legend recipe — to get absolute strength.
164:5.5 **Symmetry-averaged inference** (8 transforms per position) for evaluation
165:    and analysis modes only.
166:
167:## 6. Architecture for v2 (process layout)
168:
169:- Self-play process on the 3090 (search + inference copy of the net, fp16,
170:  BN folded, CUDA-graphed).
171:- Trainer process on the 3060 (or the 3090 if idle) reading a shared on-disk
172:  position store (memory-mapped numpy files per iteration); publishes weights
173:  every k steps; self-play reloads them between moves.
174:- Evaluator process on the 3060 running the anchor matches on new checkpoints.
175:- Expected combined effect of §1–2 on this machine: from ~30 games/s (dev1)
176:  to ~150–250 games/s at 32 sims, i.e. ~1 M games in 1–2 h.
177:
178:## 7. Verification to add before trusting v2
179:
180:- Cross-check Gumbel numerics against mctx on CPU (`pip install jax mctx`,
181:  CPU only, works on Windows): identical tree statistics for a handful of
182:  positions with fixed Gumbel samples.
183:- Golden test: v1 vs v2 search must produce identical visit counts on fixed
184:  positions with the uniform evaluator (before/after each refactor step).
185:- `torch.cuda.set_sync_debug_mode("error")` around a search in the test
