- Constraints: 100 ms/turn (1000 ms on turn 1), single core, 100 KB source; NN weights must be embedded in the source as encoded strings ([forum guidance](https://forum.codingame.com/t/winter-challenge-2024-feedback-and-strategies/205711/87)).
- What Legend bots do: bitboard MCTS with random playouts, an MCTS **solver** (proven-win backprop, citing Winands' "MCTS Solver" and "Score Bounded MCTS" - [darkhorse64](https://forum.codingame.com/t/ultimate-tic-tac-toe-puzzle-discussion/22616?page=6)), tree reuse, low exploration constants, 1-ply win checks in playouts, node pools of millions. Throughput quoted by players: ~20-25 k rollouts/turn gets you into Legend; "optimize further your bot to reach 80-90k and add a solver to make it into top 50" (darkhorse64, [page 8](https://forum.codingame.com/t/ultimate-tic-tac-toe-puzzle-discussion/22616?page=8)); a plain flat-MC bitboard example does "500,000 simulations within 99 milliseconds"; Magus allocates 5 M nodes on turn 1.
- NN bots: reCurse (CNN + MCTS, briefly #1) and jacek (NNUE-like one-hot net + MCTS-EPT) as above; pb4 and Agade pioneered NN/RL bots on CG in 2019 for other games. None published for UTTT.
- First-player advantage: "P1 has a 60% winrate for UTTT so chances are not equal" (darkhorse64, Aug 2020, [page 5](https://forum.codingame.com/t/ultimate-tic-tac-toe-puzzle-discussion/22616?page=5)); players agree "either p1 wins or a draw can be forced" and that P2 winning under optimal play is "highly improbable" (sscg13 / jacek, page 7). These are impressions from the Legend arena, not measured statistics from a fixed pair of bots.
- Opening books: several bots hardcode the first move (centre board, some square) and small precomputed books; one Gold-league write-up found caching first moves "didn't seem to matter too much" ([hodina.net](https://dev.hodina.net/how-to-create-a-gold-league-bot-on-codingame)). A theaigames-era bot ran mass Monte Carlo and put the centre opening at "about 52% chance of winning" ([royerk](https://royerk.github.io/Neural-Network-UT3/)). No Legend bot has published an opening analysis.
- Tooling: official referee [CodinGame/game-ultimate-tictactoe](https://github.com/CodinGame/game-ultimate-tictactoe) (Java, SDK) usable with [cg-brutaltester](https://github.com/dreignier/cg-brutaltester); [Agade09/CG-UTTT-Arena](https://github.com/Agade09/CG-UTTT-Arena) (C++, Linux, plays two stdin/stdout bots; its README still carries Wondev Woman boilerplate); link hub at [yduf's page](https://yduf.github.io/CG-tic-tac-toe). Public Legend-level bot sources are rare; many Gold/Silver MCTS bots are on GitHub (e.g. [Uriopass/MCTSUTTT](https://github.com/Uriopass/MCTSUTTT), [EllainYang/UTTT_bot](https://github.com/EllainYang/UTTT_bot), [sys27/UltimateTicTacToe](https://github.com/sys27/UltimateTicTacToe)).

## 4. Non-NN engines and solving attempts

- **Nelson Elhage, [ultimattt](https://github.com/nelhage/ultimattt) (Rust, Apache-2.0), write-up at [minimax.dev](https://www.minimax.dev/docs/ultimate/) (2020).** Closed-board / draw variant ([rules page](https://minimax.dev/docs/ultimate/the-game/)). Bitboards with SIMD win checks, Zobrist hashing, minimax AI, plus PN / DFPN / a parallel hybrid PN-DFPN solver with a lock-free transposition table and "critical square" positional pruning ([pruning page](https://www.minimax.dev/docs/ultimate/pruning/)). Status: solves positions "after about 20 ply ... in a few hours" on a Ryzen 3900X; estimates the full game at "a few hundred million CPU-hours", roughly $2-10 M of cloud time. No endgame database ("I currently do not see a clear way to build endgame databases"). No first-move values published. PN search treats draw as not-win, so even a finished run would only say whether X forces a win.
- **Bertholon, Geraud-Stewart, Kugelmann, Lenoir, Naccache (ENS Paris), 2020** ([arXiv:2006.02353](https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.02353)): open-board variant only - X has a forced win in at most 43 moves, O can hold out 29, and the first two moves of any optimal strategy are identified. Implication for prior art: **nothing** for the closed-board game; it establishes that the closed-board rule is what keeps UTTT interesting, and Wikipedia records the closed-board game as unsolved.
- Alpha-beta bots ([lorenzofavaro](https://github.com/lorenzofavaro/ultimate-tic-tac-toe), [Zslez](https://github.com/Zslez/Ultimate-Tic-Tac-Toe), [zesardine](https://github.com/zesardine/UltimateTicTacToeAI) with the "unstoppable" [YouTube video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BfmivoVFins), [xpe/rs-uttt](https://github.com/xpe/rs-uttt)) top out around CodinGame Silver; the game lacks a good static evaluation, which is exactly the gap a value net fills.
- No endgame tablebase, retrograde analysis, or solved-opening claim exists for the closed-board variants in either tiebreak.

## 5. Frameworks and environments

- **OpenSpiel** has `ultimate_tic_tac_toe` ([source](https://github.com/google-deepmind/open_spiel/blob/master/open_spiel/games/ultimate_tic_tac_toe/ultimate_tic_tac_toe.cc), Apache-2.0). Rules: closed boards; draw when all nine local boards are terminal; **no most-boards tiebreak**. Implementation quirks that matter for training: when sent to a closed board the player makes a separate "choose local board" action first (so a free move is two plies and the action space is 9, not 81), and `UndoAction` is a no-op. Observation tensor is 3x9x9 + current player + a 10-way forced-board one-hot. The generic `alpha_zero` example works on any registered game but DeepMind published **no UTTT AlphaZero results** ([alpha_zero docs](https://openspiel.readthedocs.io/en/latest/alpha_zero.html)).
- **Pgx** (JAX): no UTTT in its game list ([pgx README](https://github.com/sotetsuk/pgx)).
- **Ludii** includes an Ultimate Tic-Tac-Toe ruleset ([ludii.games](https://ludii.games/details.php?keyword=Ultimate+Tic-Tac-Toe)) and the Ludii-Polygames bridge can train AZ-style models on any Ludii game ([Soemers et al. 2022](https://doi.org/10.3233/icg-220197)); no UTTT results were reported.
- **LightZero**, **muzero-general**: tic-tac-toe only; UTTT exists only as the unreported fork above.
- **Datasets:** uttt.ai's 16 M MCTS-labelled positions (Drive link in its README; [Hugging Face mirror](https://huggingface.co/datasets/markstanl/u3t)). Draw-variant labels, but the game tree is identical to CodinGame's until the final position, so they remain useful for supervised warm-starting.

## 6. Published strategy analysis derived from AI

What exists is thin and prose-only:

- **uttt.ai's "strategy learned from AI"** ([blog](https://uttt.ai/blog)): open in the centre square of the centre board ("undoubtedly the best move"); O's best reply pushes play into a corner board, and the next ~8 moves stay in the corner boards; when play breaks out, "jump between the side subgames (these are the least useful to take)"; keep the position balanced and wait for a mistake; and "think twice before sending your opponent to the finished subgame (being able to choose any move from the unfinished subgames is very powerful)". Also a worked example where the network's greedy choice (win the centre board) loses and only >300 sims find the drawing move. Self-play statistics: 40-50 plies per game, ~7 legal moves per position.
- **SaltZero**: the net learns not to win local boards when that is bad, and to trap opponents so that every move gives the bot a free move.
- **nelhage's critical-square analysis**: when one player is one move from a macro win in some board, the opponent must never play into the corresponding relative square anywhere, and boards whose only open squares are such squares become forced losses - a rigorous statement of the tempo concept, used for pruning ([pruning page](https://www.minimax.dev/docs/ultimate/pruning/)).
- **Monte Carlo opening statistics** (royerk): centre opening ~52% for X under random-playout MC; a best-reply matrix, unpublished.
- **CodinGame folk knowledge**: P1 ~60% among Legend bots; game length 50-60 moves; local-board sacrifices are common.
- Human guides ([tictoe.org](https://tictoe.org/games/ultimate-tic-tac-toe), [tictactoefun](https://tictactoefun.com/blog/ultimate-strategy.html)) repeat centre-centre as best and warn about free moves, without evidence.

**Not found anywhere:** an 81-cell first-move value heatmap from a strong agent, a reply table for X's opening, a quantified value of a free move, draw/win rates by opening under either tiebreak, or any of this for the most-boards variant.

## JUDGMENT

**(a) Has anyone trained a strong AlphaZero-style agent for the closed-board + most-won-boards variant? Score: 2/10 for the exact variant, 7/10 for the closed-board family.**
Two people have almost certainly done it - reCurse (briefly #1 on CodinGame) and jacek (Legend) - but under CodinGame's 100 KB / 100 ms constraints and entirely in private. Every public agent uses the draw tiebreak: uttt.ai (10 GPU-weeks, offline two-stage, clearly strong but never benchmarked against Legend bots) and SaltZero (dense net, beat the #2 CodinGame bot 113-87 at 400 ms/move in 2020). Neither trained on the most-boards rule, and SaltZero's 48% draw rate shows that rule is not a footnote. The home-PC feasibility question is answered, though: uttt.ai on one i7 + two 2080 Ti, and reCurse's Spring-2021 bot on an i7-8700K + RTX 3080 in ~50 hours, both reached the top of their respective ladders.

**(b) Released in a usable form? Score: 6/10.**
uttt.ai: full source (Apache-2.0), weights, datasets, browser demo with analysis mode, ~700 sims/s in C++. SaltZero: source (GPL-3), weights, a stdin/stdout arbiter for bot-vs-bot matches, on a bit-rotted TF stack. tacult: MIT, C++ engine, vectorized MCTS, demo. Everything else is unusable as a baseline (no weights, no strength data, or both). The CodinGame NN bots are not released at all.

**(c) Used to analyze strategy? Score: 2/10.**
A five-bullet prose list on uttt.ai and scattered forum remarks. No heatmaps, opening trees, tempo valuation, or first-player-advantage measurement from a strong agent exists for any variant, let alone the user's.

**What remains novel or valuable in the user's project:**
1. First public AlphaZero-style agent trained on the CodinGame rules, with a calibrated strength measurement against SaltZero and a Legend-level MCTS bot through the CodinGame referee / brutaltester.
2. Any quantitative strategy output at all: first-move value map, X's opening book and O's replies, value of a free move, how often the most-boards tiebreak decides games at high level, P1 win rate under strong play - none of it exists.
3. A comparison of the two tiebreaks using the same agent (how much strategy changes when draws become most-boards wins) would be new.
4. Methodological: a modern, GPU-batched, honest-Elo pipeline for a game where every prior public attempt either had no benchmark or ran offline for ten weeks.

**Best starting points / baselines:**
- **Strongest available test opponent: SaltZero** ([farmersrice/saltzero](https://github.com/farmersrice/saltzero), GPL-3). It is the only released NN agent with an external, top-CodinGame-bot benchmark, and it ships an arbiter and a line protocol (opponent's last move in; row col out). Cost: pinning Python 3.11 + TF 2.12, and its draw-variant semantics - either play it under its rules for a strength calibration, or wrap it under CodinGame rules knowing it was not trained for them. Second: **uttt.ai's C++ NMCTS** (Apache-2.0, ~700 sims/s on GPU, weights on Drive) - probably the stronger engine but unbenchmarked; playing it against SaltZero would itself be a new result. Third: a **Legend-grade bitboard MCTS + solver** at 100 ms, rebuilt from the forum recipe (bitboards, 80-90 k rollouts, MCTS solver); no released Legend source, so this is a day's work, but it is the opponent that connects to the arena's Elo scale.
- **Code to borrow:** tacult's `utac` C++ engine (MIT, nanobind, vectorized MCTS) is the cleanest modern scaffold; uttt.ai's `utttcpp` (Apache-2.0) is proven at scale but idiosyncratic; alpha-zero-general (MIT) plus the unmerged UTTT PR is the simplest but pure-Python slow; OpenSpiel's game is clean but has the two-ply free-move quirk and no tiebreak, so a from-scratch 81-action bitboard environment matching `Referee.java` exactly is recommended. Apache-2.0 and MIT sources can be mixed freely; SaltZero's GPL-3 confines it to a separate opponent process, which is how it is meant to be used anyway.

**Windows-specific PyTorch notes**:
- **`torch.compile` on Windows**: still not officially supported - the GPU path requires Triton, which historically had no Windows build ([pytorch/pytorch#122094](https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/issues/122094), open since March 2024). Community forks ([triton-windows](https://github.com/woct0rdho/triton-windows)) now bundle TinyCC so `triton.jit` kernels work without installing MSVC, and recent releases target `torch.compile` compatibility, but this remains an unofficial, self-maintained path, not the default `pip install torch` experience.
- **CUDA graphs** are the more reliable Windows-native alternative to `torch.compile` for cutting per-step Python/launch overhead in the tight self-play inference loop, and require no compiler.
- **channels_last + AMP + TF32**: PyTorch enables TF32 for convolutions by default on Ampere; channels_last memory format is designed to pair with AMP to better feed Tensor Cores. Both are free wins to enable, though gains on a network this small (few channels, 9x9 spatial) are proportionally smaller than on ImageNet-scale ResNets - small-channel-count convolutions are documented to be more memory/launch-bound than compute-bound, which caps how much Tensor Core layout tricks alone can buy.
- **`multiprocessing` spawn overhead**: Windows has no `fork()`, so every worker process starts via `spawn` - a fresh interpreter plus pickling of everything sent to it - which is markedly slower to launch than Linux's copy-on-write `fork`. This penalizes Architecture A/C's process-per-game-worker model specifically at start-up/restart time, not steady-state throughput; keep long-lived worker pools rather than spawning per iteration.

## 3. Using two GPUs of different sizes (3090 + 3060)

There is no NVLink bridge between a 3090 and a 3060 (and consumer NVLink bridges are card-specific; a 3060 has no NVLink connector at all), so any communication between the two goes over PCIe. **Data-parallel training (DDP) across mismatched GPUs is a poor fit**: DDP synchronizes gradients every step and runs at the pace of the slowest replica, so pairing a 3090 with a ~2.5-3x slower 3060 wastes most of the 3090's throughput waiting at each all-reduce barrier - the standard multi-GPU guidance is to use identically-specced cards for DDP, and to reach for model/pipeline-sharding (FSDP) rather than DDP when memory, not compute-matching, is the constraint. Since the UTTT network is tiny (well under a GB), memory sharding is irrelevant here, so the natural pattern is not DDP at all but **role separation**, following directly from Section 2's finding that self-play is >90% of wall-clock:

- **RTX 3090  self-play inference server(s).** Give the higher-throughput card to the workload that dominates total time. Its extra ~2.5-3x compute and larger batch headroom (24GB) matter most here.
- **RTX 3060  training + evaluation/gating matches.** Gradient steps on a network this size are cheap and infrequent relative to self-play volume, so the weaker card is not a bottleneck; it can also host the periodic "new network vs. best network" evaluation matches (themselves just batched inference) without competing with self-play for the 3090.
- Alternative: run self-play workers on **both** GPUs independently (each hosting its own inference server for its own game pool, no cross-GPU communication) to add the 3060's throughput to the total, with training run in short bursts on whichever GPU is momentarily free. This adds ~30-40% more aggregate self-play throughput versus 3090-only, at the cost of more moving parts.

Either pattern avoids DDP's slowest-common-denominator problem entirely, since the two GPUs never need to agree on a gradient in lockstep.

## 4. Back-of-envelope plan for UTTT

Assumptions (stated explicitly, per the request):
- Raw NN throughput on the 3090 for a 6x64 net: **R = 200,000 evaluations/sec** (middle of the 150K-400K range derived in Section 2).
- MCTS simulations per move: **S = 100** (mid-point of the 64-200 range; Pgx used just 32 for Go 9x9, boardlaw used 64 for Hex, so 100 is a deliberately conservative middle value for UTTT's tactical, forcing-sequence character).
- Moves per game: **M = 50** (UTTT games can run up to 81 plies but commonly end earlier via a meta-board win).
- Tree reuse/caching ignored (worst case: every simulation is a fresh NN call - a real MCTS implementation with a transposition table would do somewhat better).

Arithmetic:
```
moves/sec  = R / S            = 200,000 / 100        = 2,000 moves/sec
games/sec  = moves/sec / M    = 2,000 / 50            = 40 games/sec
games/hour = games/sec x 3600 = 40 x 3600             = 144,000 games/hour
```
Sensitivity: at the low end of throughput (100K eval/sec) with heavier search (200 sims/move), games/hour drops to `100,000/200/50x3600 � 36,000`. At the high end (400K eval/sec, 64 sims/move), it rises to `400,000/64/50x3600 � 450,000`. So the plausible **range is roughly 35,000-450,000 self-play games/hour**, i.e., a UTTT self-play generator on this 3090 is very unlikely to be starved for data at any reasonable target game count - the reference-point table above shows strong small-game agents built from 50,000 (Othello, world-class-competitive) to ~1.3M games (Go 9x9, Pachi-competitive, derived as 52M frames � ~40 moves/game for the Pgx baseline checkpoint) to tens of millions (Hex, provably-near-perfect). Even at the pessimistic 36,000 games/hour end, reaching:
- **50,000 games** (Othello/OLIVAW-strength anchor): **~1.4 hours**
- **~1.3M games** (Go-9x9/Pachi-strength anchor): **~36 hours (1.5 days)**
- **10M games** (approaching Hex-style near-solved play): **~11.5 days**

The realistic bottleneck for reaching *strong* (not provably perfect) UTTT play is therefore not self-play throughput but training-loop wall-clock overhead (Python orchestration, checkpoint I/O, evaluation-match gating, hyperparameter iteration) and engineering time to get Architecture B/C's vectorized self-play working correctly - the same lesson the `boardlaw` paper draws about the "takeoff" and "plateau" of its compute-vs-Elo curves: getting from random play to *decent* play is cheap and fast; the last few hundred Elo toward provably perfect play costs disproportionately more (roughly 7x more compute per additional board-size increment, or ~10x compute per +500 Elo in the linear regime). A pragmatic target for this machine is **low hundreds of thousands of self-play games over a few hours to at most one or two days**, which the arithmetic above shows is comfortably within budget if the self-play engine hits even the low end of the estimated throughput range - meaning the real project risk is implementation correctness and iteration speed, not raw GPU-hours.

## 5. Software recommendations for Windows without a compiler

- **Numba**: confirmed to work without any C/C++ compiler or even a system LLVM install - `llvmlite` (Numba's LLVM binding) ships LLVM statically linked into its wheel, so `pip install numba` is sufficient on its own. This makes Architecture C (Numba-JIT'd UTTT rules/legal-move generation + batched PyTorch inference) fully viable on this machine as-is.
- **Visual Studio Build Tools**: worth installing only if a genuine C++ extension (e.g., a hand-written bitboard MCTS engine, or to unlock `torch.compile`'s CPU backend and the unofficial `triton-windows` GPU path) becomes necessary later. For the Architecture B/C plans above, it is not a prerequisite - Numba and stock PyTorch cover the need. If installed regardless (cheap, ~a few GB), it also unblocks building any future Cython/pybind11 UTTT engine without reaching for WSL.
- **PyTorch CUDA wheel**: for an RTX 3090 (Ampere, compute capability 8.6), both `cu126` and `cu128` wheels are fully supported; `cu128` is the more future-proof current default (newer cuDNN, actively the baseline for newer ecosystem packages) and is safe on Windows for full cuDNN functionality - pick `cu128` unless a specific dependency pins `cu126`.
- **Pin versions**: lock `torch`, `numba`/`llvmlite`, and CUDA driver version together in a requirements file/lockfile from the start; Numba/llvmlite releases are sensitive to Python version compatibility, and PyTorch CUDA wheels are sensitive to driver version floors - an unpinned upgrade of either mid-project is a common source of silent slowdowns or crashes.
- **Monitoring**: TensorBoard (bundled with PyTorch via `torch.utils.tensorboard`, zero extra infra) or Weights & Biases (richer dashboards, used by several of the small AlphaZero repos surveyed above, e.g. [zhihanyang2022/alpha-zero](https://github.com/zhihanyang2022/alpha-zero)) for loss curves, self-play throughput, and Elo-over-time plots.
- **Rating tracking**: BayesElo (used by both the original AlphaZero paper and the [Pgx paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.17503)) or TrueSkill for tracking generation-over-generation strength via periodic gating matches - both are standard, well-documented choices; BayesElo specifically models draw rate and first-move advantage, relevant since UTTT is not symmetric move-for-move.
- **Replay buffer storage**: NumPy structured arrays (or a plain memory-mapped `.npy`/`.dat` file of fixed-size records: board planes + policy target + value target) are the simplest durable format for a buffer of millions of small UTTT positions and avoid pickling overhead; with 128GB RAM, buffers of several million positions can likely stay fully in memory, sidestepping disk I/O entirely for a game this small.
- **WSL2 for JAX/pgx/mctx GPU**: **yes**, installing WSL2 would unlock the JAX ecosystem (Pgx's own environments, DeepMind's `mctx` for batched MCTS) with GPU passthrough, since JAX's Windows-native GPU support is far behind its Linux support. Trade-offs: WSL2 GPU compute is reported at roughly 5-15% overhead versus native Linux for typical workloads (with reports of up to ~33% for some heavy workloads), file I/O across the Windows/WSL boundary is a known extra penalty if datasets sit on an NTFS-mounted drive rather than the WSL ext4 filesystem, and it adds a second environment (drivers, CUDA toolkit versions, Python env) to maintain in parallel with the native Windows PyTorch setup. Given this project's existing investment in a PyTorch CUDA 12.6 setup, the pragmatic recommendation is to build Architecture B/C directly in PyTorch on native Windows first, and treat a WSL2 + JAX/pgx/mctx port as a fallback only if PyTorch self-play throughput proves to be a genuine bottleneck against the Section 4 estimates.

## Recommended architecture for this machine

**Architecture C (Numba-JIT'd UTTT rules + batched-tensor MCTS + PyTorch inference server), with role-separated dual GPUs**: run the self-play loop as a pool of long-lived worker processes (avoiding per-iteration `spawn` cost) whose game logic and move-legality checks are Numba-compiled, feeding leaf-evaluation requests into a batched inference server pinned to the RTX 3090; use CUDA graphs (not `torch.compile`, which lacks solid Windows GPU support) to shrink per-batch launch overhead; enable TF32 and channels_last by default (cheap, no downside for this net size); run training minibatches and network-vs-network gating matches on the RTX 3060, avoiding DDP entirely since the two cards never need to synchronize gradients. If early profiling shows the Numba/Python self-play loop falling well short of the ~150K-400K eval/sec ceiling estimated in Section 2, migrate the hot path toward a more fully tensorized Architecture B (batched game-state tensors + `vmap`-style MCTS) before considering a WSL2/JAX rewrite.

## Key takeaways

1. Self-play, not training, consumes >90% of AlphaZero wall-clock time on small games - confirmed directly in the Pgx paper's own Go 9x9 experiment.
2. The best concrete anchor for UTTT is Pgx's Go 9x9 run: a 6x128 ResNet, 32 sims/move, reached a Pachi-competitive baseline in 8.6 GPU-hours on a single A100 (~3,400 positions/sec end-to-end, ~1.1x105 raw NN evals/sec).
3. Scaling that anchor down to a 6x64 net on an RTX 3090 gives an estimated **150,000-400,000 raw NN evaluations/sec**, which is an extrapolation, not a measured number - validate with a local microbenchmark early.
4. Small-game reference points span three orders of magnitude in self-play games needed: ~50,000 for competitive-with-strong-humans (Othello/OLIVAW) up to tens of millions for provably-near-perfect play (Hex 9x9 boardlaw).
5. No published, benchmarked UTTT-specific AlphaZero compute numbers exist; UTTT must be positioned by analogy to Go 9x9/Hex 9x9 (same board footprint) pending this project's own measurements.
6. Fully vectorized self-play (Architecture B, pgx/mctx-style, or Architecture C's Numba-hybrid) is 1-2 orders of magnitude faster than CPU-tree-search-per-game (Architecture A) for a game this small and cheap to simulate.
7. `torch.compile` GPU support does not work out-of-the-box on Windows (Triton dependency); CUDA graphs are the practical, compiler-free alternative for cutting kernel-launch overhead.
8. Numba requires no C/C++ compiler on Windows (LLVM is statically bundled in `llvmlite`'s wheel) - Visual Studio Build Tools are optional, not required, for the recommended architecture.
9. DDP across a 3090+3060 is a bad fit (no NVLink, ~2.5-3x speed mismatch forces every step to the slower card's pace); role-separating self-play (3090) from training/eval (3060) sidesteps the problem entirely.
10. Back-of-envelope arithmetic (200K eval/sec, 100 sims/move, 50 moves/game) yields ~144,000 self-play games/hour, meaning even a multi-million-game training target is plausibly reachable in **hours to a few days**, not weeks - the dominant project risk is implementation/engineering time, not raw compute.

